# The frozen cross-language crypto vectors are bytes, not text.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set on the machines this is developed on, and with
# no attribute here git rewrites every LF to CRLF on checkout. That changes
# the file's SHA-256, and this file's SHA-256 is asserted — by
# `CryptoVectorTests.cs` here and by `crypto_vectors_test.dart` in WLOS_App,
# against the same constant.
#
# The digest exists to pin byte-for-byte agreement between the .NET and Dart
# implementations of the envelope and the recovery signature. A digest that
# changes depending on how the file reached the disk pins nothing:
#
#   what git stores (LF)          6575 bytes   695a3856...   <- asserted
#   after a Windows checkout      6702 bytes   3e4786fc...
#   after `sed 's/$/\r/'`         6703 bytes   6f0d83b3...
#
# Three identities for one file. It matters more on this side than on the
# client: a mismatch here reads as "the .NET implementation disagrees with
# Dart", which is the one conclusion these vectors exist to make impossible to
# reach by accident. `-text` tells git never to convert it.
#
# **An existing working copy that already has CRLF stays red.** The attribute
# governs checkout, not what is already on disk. The remedy is to re-checkout
# that one file:
#
#   git checkout -- tests/Maren.Tests/wlos_crypto_vectors.json
#
# Scoped to this file deliberately rather than set repo-wide. A blanket
# `* text=auto` would renormalise every file in the repository in one commit,
# which is a large diff to review for a one-file problem — and this repository
# holds SQL scripts whose line endings nobody has had reason to think about.
tests/Maren.Tests/wlos_crypto_vectors.json -text
